## Vercel Web Analytics Configuration

Successfully configured Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js project following the latest official documentation.

### Changes Made

**Modified:**
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Updated Analytics import to use the Next.js-specific package path

### What Was Done

1. **Fetched Latest Documentation**: Retrieved the current Vercel Analytics quickstart guide from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart to ensure using the most up-to-date installation instructions.

2. **Discovered Existing Installation**: Found that `@vercel/analytics` (v2.0.1) was already installed as a dependency in package.json.

3. **Updated Import Path**: The Analytics component was already integrated in the root layout, but was importing from `@vercel/analytics/react`. According to the official Vercel documentation for Next.js App Router projects, the recommended import path is `@vercel/analytics/next`. Updated the import to follow this framework-specific best practice.

4. **Verified Implementation**: 
   - Confirmed the `<Analytics />` component is properly placed in the root layout (src/app/layout.tsx)
   - Component is correctly positioned within the `<body>` tag alongside SpeedInsights
   - Build completed successfully with no errors
   - Package-lock.json updated during dependency installation

### Technical Details

- **Framework**: Next.js 16.3.4 (App Router)
- **Package Version**: @vercel/analytics v2.0.1
- **Import Path**: `@vercel/analytics/next` (Next.js-specific, as per official docs)
- **Component Placement**: Root layout file at src/app/layout.tsx

### Why This Implementation

According to the official Vercel documentation, Next.js projects using the App Router should import Analytics from `@vercel/analytics/next` rather than the generic `/react` path. This ensures optimal integration with Next.js-specific features and proper server/client component handling.

The Analytics component is correctly positioned in the root layout's body tag, ensuring it will track pageviews across all routes in the application.

### Verification Steps Completed

✅ Dependencies installed (npm install)
✅ Build verified successful (npm run build)
✅ TypeScript compilation passed
✅ All 14 routes compiled successfully
✅ Package-lock.json updated

### Notes

- The project already had Vercel Analytics installed, so no package installation was needed beyond the initial npm install to set up the development environment
- The linter script has a configuration issue (unrelated to this change) but the build process includes TypeScript type checking which passed successfully
- No tests were available in the project to run
